# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

def blocks():
    return [
        {"t": "h1", "text": "Day 8 — .NET Framework to modern .NET"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Outcome: you can present a migration a manager can fund and an engineer can start on Monday. The target runtime is .NET 10, because .NET 8 support ends in November 2026."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Time box (3.5 hours)"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "40 min: inventory list and the strangler picture.",
            "90 min: questions aloud, using a real system if you have one.",
            "50 min: write a six-step plan with risks, owners, and what you will not rewrite.",
            "30 min: EF6 to EF Core gaps, spoken as a table.",
        ]},
        {"t": "note", "title": "The lead's opening line", "text": "I would not rewrite the system in a branch for nine months. I would inventory the blockers, put a facade in front of the surface we must keep stable, move one vertical slice to .NET 10, run both, and delete the old slice only when the new one has the same logs, the same auth, and a rollback. The goal is an empty Framework footprint on a date, not a heroic cutover weekend."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Concept block"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Modern .NET is cross-platform, uses the ASP.NET Core pipeline, the SDK-style project, PackageReference, and appsettings plus environment variables plus Key Vault. .NET Framework is Windows-first, with System.Web, Global.asax, web.config, OWIN in some apps, and often EF6 or ADO.NET.",
            "The .NET Upgrade Assistant and the API analyzer find APIs that do not exist on .NET 10. They do not find behavioral changes or a bad module boundary. The lead still reads the report.",
            "A class library can multi-target net48 and net10 for a bridge period if the code stays on the shared surface. That lets old and new hosts share a pure domain library. It is a temporary state with an end date.",
            "ASP.NET to ASP.NET Core replaces System.Web. HttpContext is different. Session, cache, and authentication modules move to middleware and services. Web Forms does not come along. MVC views can move with work. Web API controllers move more naturally.",
            "WCF does not ship as a full equivalent. CoreWCF keeps some SOAP services alive on modern .NET. A public contract that you can change should become REST or gRPC. I do not promise every WCF binding will appear.",
            "EF6 uses an older model, sometimes EDMX. EF Core uses code-based models, different lazy loading, different interceptors, and migrations you own in code. Some queries translate differently. You test the expensive queries against SQL.",
            "Authentication often moves from Windows auth or OWIN cookies to Entra ID and JWT for an API, or to ASP.NET Core cookie auth for a site. I do not leave anonymous endpoints by accident during the port.",
            "System.Drawing, COM, the registry, Event Log assumptions, Windows services, and impersonation are the usual blockers. I list them in week one.",
            "The strangler fig puts a routing layer in front. Some routes go to the new host. The rest stay. IIS, YARP, or a gateway can do that routing. Data compatibility is the real constraint.",
        ]},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Give me your migration plan.",
         "a": "Week zero is an inventory: projects, target framework, NuGet packages, System.Web usage, WCF, EF6 or EDMX, auth type, Windows-only APIs, scheduled jobs, and how we deploy. I run the upgrade analyzer and I sit with someone who knows the ugly paths. I pick .NET 10 as the target and I freeze new features that deepen Framework-only APIs. I add characterization tests around the slices we will move, at the HTTP boundary if unit tests do not exist. I move a pure class library first, then one vertical API slice, behind the existing URL via a reverse proxy. I ship that slice to production traffic, small, with the old path still available. I repeat. Schema changes use expand/contract so both runtimes can work. I remove a Framework project only when nothing calls it. I report percent of traffic on the new runtime, not percent of projects converted.",
         "lead": "Traffic percent is the metric I show a steering group. Converted projects can be the easy libraries while the revenue path is still on Framework. I have seen a status deck that said 70 percent migrated because seven of ten projects were class libraries nobody deployed alone."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What usually blocks a Web API migration?",
         "a": "HTTP modules and handlers that quietly do auth, logging, or tenant resolution. Those become middleware, and if I miss one, the new host is missing a control. WCF clients generated from old references. Binary serialization and Remoting, which should become an explicit contract. Configuration in ConfigurationManager and web.config transforms, which become the options pattern. Static HttpContext.Current, which has no place in ASP.NET Core; the context is passed or accessed through IHttpContextAccessor sparingly. App-pool identity assumptions, which become a managed identity. Blocking synchronous IO that happened to work at low volume. And packages that were never updated past a .NET Framework build. I classify each blocker as replace, wrap for now, or stop the slice.",
         "lead": "I publish the blocker list with an owner and a decision. A migration without that list becomes a long branch. The list is also how I say no to a rewrite of the reporting module that is not on the critical path."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you move EF6 to EF Core without a surprise in production?",
         "a": "I treat it as a new data layer, not a namespace rename. I recreate the model in code, map keys, required relationships, delete behaviors, and column types explicitly, and I compare the schema EF Core wants with the schema that exists. I do not let it create a parallel database by accident. I port the hot queries first and run them against a copy of production statistics or a realistic dataset, because translation differences change plans. Lazy loading is opt-in and different; I prefer explicit loads and projections. EDMX does not come forward. I keep EF6 in the old process and EF Core in the new process during the strangler, both pointed at the same database only with strict rules: no silent model drift, and migrations owned by one side. Spatial types, table-per-type inheritance, and some complex mappings need an explicit check. I test concurrency tokens and transactions, not only CRUD.",
         "lead": "One owner of the schema during the overlap is non-negotiable. Two migration histories on one database is how a Friday deploy drops a column the old app still reads."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you migrate authentication safely?",
         "a": "I write down the current mechanism: Windows integrated, forms cookie, OWIN JWT, custom headers, or certificates. I map every audience: browser, partner, and internal job. For a new API I prefer Entra ID and bearer tokens, with policies that match today's roles. I run both authenticators during overlap if clients cannot all move, and I log which scheme succeeded. I test 401 and 403 on every moved route, plus one cross-tenant case. I do not trust a ported [Authorize] until I see a test fail closed. Secrets in web.config move to Key Vault references. Certificates get an owner and an expiry alert. A Windows-identity flow that relied on impersonation to SQL is a design change: the user's identity stays in the token, and the app's managed identity connects to SQL, with authorization enforced in the API."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What do you test, given the old system has few tests?",
         "a": "I start with characterization tests on the HTTP contract of the slice I am moving: status code, important headers, and the JSON shape for the main success and the main failure. I add golden tests around billing calculations and any code that has caused an incident. I add an integration test that hits SQL for the migrated query. I do not stop the migration to unit-test the entire legacy solution. I do not accept a rewrite of a slice with no before-and-after contract check. For the proxy, I test routing rules: this path goes to the new host, that path stays, and a bad route fails in a way we can see. Load on the migrated slice is a test, because sync-over-async and missing indexes show up there.",
         "lead": "I sequence tests by money and embarrassment. The payment path gets a characterization test before the admin dropdown does. That is a lead tradeoff I will say in the interview."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you estimate, and what do you refuse to rewrite?",
         "a": "I estimate the inventory and the first slice, not the whole estate. The first slice teaches us the real cost of auth, EF, and deployment. After it is in production I estimate the rest in slices, with a range. I refuse to rewrite code we do not understand, code with no contract, and code the business is about to replace. I wrap those and put an expiry on the wrap. I also refuse a second feature program that lands only on Framework while we migrate. New work goes to the new runtime unless there is a dated exception. I keep a risk register: shared database, unknown module, key-person knowledge, package with no modern build, and a rollback that requires schema compatibility.",
         "lead": "A single number for an 80-project solution is fiction. I give a funded first slice, a risk register, and a cadence. That is the estimate a tech lead can defend."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How does rollback work six weeks into a strangler?",
         "a": "Routing rollback sends the slice's traffic to the old host. That only works if the old host can still read and write the current schema, which is why we expanded the schema and did not rename or drop in the same release. Data written by the new service must be readable by the old service, or I have a compensating job I have rehearsed. Configuration and feature flags can disable a new behavior without a deploy. I do not need to roll back the class libraries that both hosts share if they stayed compatible. I rehearse the route switch in a lower environment. If I cannot describe rollback, the slice is too big.",
         "lead": "I put rollback on the release checklist next to the migration script. The person on call should not invent it at midnight."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Which .NET features do you adopt during the move, and which do you postpone?",
         "a": "I adopt the SDK-style project, nullable reference types on new code, generic hosting, the options pattern, structured logging, and EF Core migrations owned by the pipeline. I adopt managed identity if the app is moving to Azure as part of this work. I postpone a microservices split, a new UI framework, and a domain rewrite. The migration's job is to get onto a supported runtime with the same business behavior and a safer deploy. A cleaner module boundary inside the new host is welcome when it falls out of the slice. A new architecture program disguised as a migration is how the date slips and Framework stays in production past support.",
         "lead": "I say this to stakeholders in one sentence: supported runtime first, structural improvement only where the slice already has to change. That sentence has saved more migrations than a pattern catalog."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Blockers you should be able to classify"},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Blocker", "A lead's classification"], "rows": [
            ["System.Web HttpModule", "Replace with middleware in the slice. Test the behavior it used to own."],
            ["WCF SOAP the partner still calls", "CoreWCF as a bridge, or keep that host until the partner can move. Do not block the rest of the API."],
            ["EDMX", "Replace with a code model for the migrated slice. Compare schema. Do not auto-drop."],
            ["Web Forms page", "Leave it, or rebuild that page as a separate decision. It is not a class-library port."],
            ["AppSettings in web.config", "Options classes, environment overrides, Key Vault for secrets."],
            ["Windows service", "Worker Service on .NET 10, or a Function if it is a schedule or a queue."],
            ["COM or registry", "Wrap and isolate. It may force that component to stay on Windows even when the API moves."],
            ["Shared SQL login as db_owner", "Split runtime and migration identities as part of the first production slice."],
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Evening drill"},
        {"t": "b", "numbered": True, "items": [
            "Write a one-page plan for a system you know, even if you change the names. Include the first slice and the metric.",
            "Name three APIs or libraries that would not port cleanly, and classify each.",
            "Explain expand/contract plus route rollback in under two minutes.",
        ]},
        {"t": "break"},
    ]
